.docname {Architecture}
.include {docs}

The repository is organized around one library, two executables, and a single
example configuration file.

.filetree
    - Cargo.toml
    - Cargo.lock
    - README.md
    - simulation.example.toml
    - assets/
      - shaders/
      - sprites/
    - benches/
      - board_refresh.rs
    - docs/
    - notes/
    - scripts/
      - package_gui.sh
    - src/
      - bin/
        - gui.rs
      - **lib.rs**
      - main.rs
    - tests/
      - seeded_e2e_snapshots.rs

## Modules

.repolink {src/lib.rs} {blob/main/src/lib.rs} holds the simulation model,
configuration loading and parsing, the random number wrapper, the simulation
rules, and the rule tests. It is the only place the rules live, so both front
ends behave identically.

.repolink {src/main.rs} {blob/main/src/main.rs} holds command-line parsing,
board rendering, turn-summary printing, sleeping between turns, and the CLI
tests.

.repolink {src/bin/gui.rs} {blob/main/src/bin/gui.rs} holds the Bevy visual
simulation: the board, a `bevy_feathers` settings sidebar, the population
history chart, the food overlay, cell editing, illustrated sprites, and count
badges for crowded cells.
.repolink {notes/bevy-board-rendering.md} {blob/main/notes/bevy-board-rendering.md}
records its design and the trade-offs behind it.

`assets/` holds the sprite artwork with its generation prompts and the cell
shader. All of it is embedded into the executable at build time, so a packaged
build needs no asset directory beside it.

`target/` is generated by Cargo and ignored by Git.

## Core types

| Type | Role |
|------|------|
| `Board` | Cells, creatures, and the `refresh` call that advances one turn |
| `Random` | Seeded or OS-seeded wrapper over `rand_chacha::ChaCha12Rng` |
| `AphidParams`, `LadybugParams`, `FoodParams` | Tunable probabilities, with defaults |
| `BoardSummary`, `TurnStats` | Population, food, and per-turn birth and death counts |
| `CellSnapshot`, `CreatureSnapshot` | Read-only views for rendering and reporting |

Configuration moves through `parse_simulation_config`,
`load_configured_board`, `format_simulation_config`, and
`save_configured_board`, which keeps the TOML format in one place for both the
CLI and the GUI.

## Dependencies

| Crate | Purpose |
|-------|---------|
| `bevy` | Realtime GUI visualization, windowing, and UI widgets |
| `clap` | Command-line parsing and help output |
| `rand` | Seeded and unseeded random number generation |
| `serde` | TOML deserialization |
| `toml` | Runtime configuration parsing |
| `criterion` | Benchmark harness (dev dependency) |

Everything except `bevy` is small. Bevy is a game engine and brings a large
dependency tree with it, which makes the first build slow; later builds are
incremental.

## The `gui` feature gate

Bevy sits behind the default-on `gui` feature because only the GUI needs it.
Building without default features leaves the library, the CLI, the benches, and
the tests untouched and skips Bevy entirely: 49 crates instead of the full
tree.

```sh
cargo build --no-default-features
```

The `gui` binary declares `required-features = ["gui"]`, so asking for it
without the feature reports that rather than failing to compile. CI runs
`cargo check --locked --no-default-features --all-targets` to keep the gate
honest.

## Release profile

```toml
[profile.release]
lto = "thin"
codegen-units = 1
strip = true
```

## Engineering notes

`notes/` holds records of how the project was built, as plain Markdown kept
outside the documentation site. Numbered **decision records** state the context
a decision was made in, what was decided, and what it cost; they are written
after the fact from the commit history and are superseded rather than edited.
Longer **design sketches** keep descriptive names.

| # | Decision |
|---|----------|
| .repolink {0001} {blob/main/notes/0001-deterministic-seeded-simulation.md} | A seeded random wrapper makes every run reproducible |
| .repolink {0002} {blob/main/notes/0002-phased-turn-order.md} | A turn is seven phases over a start-of-turn snapshot |
| .repolink {0003} {blob/main/notes/0003-toml-configuration.md} | TOML replaces the positional `.conf` files |
| .repolink {0004} {blob/main/notes/0004-xdg-config-location.md} | The config lives in the XDG config home and is created on demand |
| .repolink {0005} {blob/main/notes/0005-food-regeneration.md} | Food regrows slowly and is capped per cell |
| .repolink {0006} {blob/main/notes/0006-gui-writes-the-shared-config.md} | The GUI edits the board and saves to the CLI's config file |
| .repolink {0007} {blob/main/notes/0007-macos-app-bundle.md} | A shell script packages the GUI as a macOS app bundle |
| .repolink {0008} {blob/main/notes/0008-benchmark-as-performance-guard.md} | A Criterion benchmark guards `Board::refresh` |
| .repolink {0009} {blob/main/notes/0009-flat-creature-storage.md} | Creatures live in flat vectors indexed by ID |
| .repolink {0010} {blob/main/notes/0010-macroquad-to-bevy.md} | The GUI moves from macroquad to Bevy |
| .repolink {0011} {blob/main/notes/0011-optional-gui-feature.md} | Bevy sits behind an optional, default-on `gui` feature |
| .repolink {0012} {blob/main/notes/0012-github-actions-ci.md} | CI runs on GitHub Actions instead of Woodpecker |
| .repolink {0013} {blob/main/notes/0013-docs-deployed-to-github-pages.md} | The documentation site deploys to GitHub Pages from CI |

The design sketch
.repolink {notes/bevy-board-rendering.md} {blob/main/notes/bevy-board-rendering.md}
is the deep companion to records 0010 and 0011.
